INT. APPELLATE COURTHOUSE - JUDGES' ROBING ROOM - MORNING

Marble sinks. A rack of black robes. A stained-glass window throws red and gold on the floor. The air smells of starch.

JUDGE PHILOMENA ARCHIBALD-NWOSU, 60s, silver hair pinned under a cap, buttons her robe at the mirror. A YOUNG MAN in a rumpled suit slips in through the side door, clutching a leather folio. He freezes when he sees her.

YOUNG MAN
Oh. Sorry. I thought this was the clerk's office.

PHILOMENA
It's the robing room. Third door down.

YOUNG MAN
Right. Of course.

He doesn't leave. She meets his eyes in the mirror and turns slowly.

PHILOMENA
Are you lost, or are you someone I should know?

YOUNG MAN
I'm counsel for the appellant. In Whitlock versus the State.

PHILOMENA
Name?

YOUNG MAN
Gideon.

PHILOMENA
Gideon what?

GIDEON
Gideon Okafor-Lindgren.

PHILOMENA
You'll want to hold that for the bench, Mr. Okafor-Lindgren. Not for a judge adjusting her collar.

GIDEON
Yes, ma'am. Your Honor. Sorry.

She turns back to the mirror. Fastens the last button. Her fingers pause.

PHILOMENA
Whitlock. The arson.

GIDEON
Yes.

PHILOMENA
You argue today?

GIDEON
Ten o'clock.

PHILOMENA
Your first appellate?

He hesitates. Looks at his folio.

GIDEON
Is it that obvious?

PHILOMENA
You walked into a judges' dressing room with your tie untucked.

He glances at his tie. Flips it back under his jacket. She watches him struggle with it and her expression softens.

PHILOMENA (CONT'D)
Come here.

GIDEON
Your Honor?

PHILOMENA
Come here.

He steps forward. She tucks the tie, smooths his lapel with both hands, adjusts his collar. The way a mother would.

PHILOMENA (CONT'D)
That's better. Now. Do not tell me your theory of the case.

GIDEON
I wasn't going to.

PHILOMENA
You were.

GIDEON
...I was.

She nods. Steps back. Her eyes flicker over his face.

PHILOMENA
Mr. Okafor-Lindgren. Who taught you to argue?

GIDEON
Judge Waverly Bishop.

Her hands go still.

PHILOMENA
Waverly Bishop.

GIDEON
Yes, ma'am. Before she passed.

PHILOMENA
I know. I sat beside her for eleven years.

A beat. The light shifts through the glass. Gideon, uncertain, shifts his feet.

GIDEON
Is something wrong?

PHILOMENA
She wrote me a letter. Before she died. About a young man in her clinic. She said, "He will lose his first appeal, and it will be the best thing for him."

GIDEON
She wrote you about me?

PHILOMENA
She wrote me about justice. You were the example.

Gideon sags against the sink. Philomena gathers her gloves.

PHILOMENA (CONT'D)
I won't tell you how to argue. But I will tell you this. I read your brief last night.

GIDEON
And?

PHILOMENA
It was good enough to be sad about.

GIDEON
(laughing, almost choking)
Thank you?

PHILOMENA
Don't. Not yet.

She walks to the door. Stops with her hand on the knob.

PHILOMENA (CONT'D)
Judge Philomena Archibald-Nwosu does not take shortcuts. If you come before me, you earn it.

She exits. The door closes softly. Gideon stands alone in the red light, tie perfectly knotted.

INT. APPELLATE COURTROOM - LATER

Packed gallery. Three judges at the bench. Gideon at the lectern, folio open, knuckles white.

JUDGE ARCHIBALD-NWOSU
Counsel. You may proceed.

He takes a breath.

GIDEON
May it please the court.

PHILOMENA
Mr. Okafor-Lindgren, please. Your Honor will do.
